## Dead Ends
None of the blog, z-lab reference repository, SGLang implementation, vLLM implementation, or public model card publishes selector labels, a loss, negative construction, initialization, optimizer schedule, or convergence procedure. The sources therefore cannot confirm or replace TorchSpec's training objective. A strict top-K-hit-only loss was not adopted because a from-scratch draft has almost no initial top-K recall and would starve selector gradients. Adding a TorchSpec serving path walk was unnecessary because TorchSpec exports the model and the serving engines own the walk and lossless verification.

## Hints for Future Agents
Calling CandidateSelector.score_candidates without training_successor_ids uses the strict top-K candidate set that public inference uses. Supplying training_successor_ids is a TorchSpec-only training augmentation: it replaces the weakest unary candidate only when the gold successor is absent. Public serving builds all K-by-K edges, starts slot zero from the verified anchor, and uses the selected candidate index as the predecessor row at the next slot. TorchSpec's teacher-forced loss scores the row for the gold predecessor instead. Do not claim private training-recipe parity. DFlash2Config must dispatch before DFlashConfig, packed convolutions must reset at block boundaries, and official codebook checkpoint keys omit .weight.

## Codebase Discoveries
The Inco blog states S_t(a,b)=U_t(b)+<A(a) elementwise H(h_t),B(b)> and describes a path walk from the last verified token. z-lab/model.py implements that score sequentially. SGLang dflash.py and vLLM qwen3_dflash2.py precompute the full [batch, slot, predecessor-K, successor-K] lattice, while their speculators walk one realized row per slot and preserve proposal probabilities for lossless sampling verification. All public implementations apply output_multiplier and optional final_logit_softcapping to unary values. The public sources are inference-only with respect to selector supervision.

## Open Questions
The candidate-augmented teacher-forced selector objective remains a TorchSpec design that needs a real CUDA multi-rank training and convergence run. Whether the private DFlash2 recipe uses gold insertion, staged training, strict top-K masking, path-level supervision, or another objective remains unknown. The open vLLM pull request also needs its reported concurrent-serving crash resolved before it can serve as runtime validation.
